{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["And Timna was a concubine — I saw in Shocher Tov: ותמנע is connected also to the preceding verse, for so it is in Chronicles: צפי וגעתם וקנז ותמנע ועמלק. And afterward it says concerning the sons of Seir: ואחות לוטן תמנע. Like אחות נביות, אחות אהרן, as I explained. [And likewise here, 'and the sons of Eliphaz were Teman,' it says 'Omar, Zepho, Gatam, Kenaz, and Timna.']" "And Timna was a concubine — the first occurrence lists her among the sons of Eliphaz, and the second is a female: ואחות לוטן תמנע. And similarly in Joshua: ועתה חלק את הארץ הזאת בנחלה לתשעת השבטים וחצי השבט המנשה עמו הראובני והגדי לקחו נחלתם אשר נתן להם משה בעבר הירדן מזרחה. Perforce a second verse is missing something, for it should have said: וחצי שבט המנשה — 'also took their inheritance.' Therefore one must say that this verse continues onto the end of the verse in which is written וחצי שבט המנשה, even though the verses speak of the two halves of the tribe of Menasheh — for one of them Moshe apportioned and for the second Joshua apportioned. And I, Shmuel, found a third instance of the same pattern in Chronicles, in the families of the sons of Yehonatan son of Sha'ul: ובני מיכה פיתון ומלך ותארע ואחז. ואחז הוליד את יהועדה ויהועדה הוליד את עלמת וגו׳. And that passage is repeated and duplicated elsewhere, the beginning of the repeated passage being ובגבעון ישבו and the end of the passage אלה בני אצל. And there it is written: ובני מיכה פיתון ומלך ותחרע. ואחז הוליד וגו׳. Perforce ואחז is missing from this verse, for it should have written ותחרע ואחז, ואחז הוליד — except that the phrase ואחז הוליד continues from the verse above, as though written: ובני מיכה פיתון ומלך ותחרע ואחז, ואחז הוליד וגו׳ — for so it is written in the first passage."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {1 "The bracketed text in comment 0 appears to be a later marginal gloss; it translates the Hebrew forms cited (Omar, Zepho, etc.) as they stand in the manuscript. In comment 1, the phrase עולה על סוף הפסוק / עולה על פסוק שלמעלה is rendered as 'continues onto the end of the verse' / 'continues from the verse above,' Rashbam's technical term for a word that syntactically belongs with an adjacent verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:46:28.552197964Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}